---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SectionLabel from '../../components/SectionLabel.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
---
<Base title="The lab" description="Break a tool contract and inspect the result. An interactive, browser-local explanation of schema failures and honest validation.">
  <section class="page-intro container"><SectionLabel number="02" label="THE LAB" extra="CURIOSITY, WITH THE LID OFF" /><h1>Change a thing.<br /><em>See what breaks.</em></h1><div class="page-intro-bottom"><p>Small, hands-on explanations of the ideas inside the work. No setup. Just a question, an input, and something to learn.</p><span class="mono">EXPERIMENT_001 / AGENT TOOLING</span></div></section>
  <section class="container lab-page-section" aria-labelledby="experiment-title"><div class="experiment-heading"><div><span class="mono lime">THE SCHEMA FAULT LAB</span><h2 id="experiment-title">Can the agent trust this contract?</h2></div><a class="text-link" href="/work/schema-sentinel/">The project behind it <Icon /></a></div><p class="experiment-description">A tool expects a <code>Location</code>. Its schema points to a definition. What if that definition disappears between systems?</p>
    <div class="lab-fixtures js-only" role="group" aria-label="Choose a schema fixture"><button data-fixture="broken" aria-pressed="true"><span>01</span> Break the reference</button><button data-fixture="repaired" aria-pressed="false"><span>02</span> Restore the definition</button><button data-fixture="unsupported" aria-pressed="false"><span>03</span> Cross the boundary</button></div>
    <div class="schema-lab"><div class="lab-input-panel"><div class="lab-panel-label mono"><span>INPUT / TOOLS.JSON</span><span data-fixture-label>DANGLING REFERENCE</span></div><pre class="lab-code"><code data-fixture-code>{JSON.stringify({ name: 'get_weather', inputSchema: { type: 'object', properties: { location: { $ref: '#/$defs/Location' } }, required: ['location'] } }, null, 2)}</code></pre><div class="lab-input-footer"><span class="mono">CURATED FIXTURE · READ-ONLY</span><button class="button button-lime js-only" data-run-fixture>Inspect this schema <Icon name="play" size={16} /></button></div></div><div class="lab-output-panel"><div class="lab-panel-label mono"><span>OUTPUT / THE VERDICT</span><span>LOCAL EXPLAINER</span></div><div class="lab-result" data-lab-result role="status" aria-live="polite"><span class="result-symbol" aria-hidden="true">↳</span><h3>The interesting part<br />is one click away.</h3><p>Inspect the schema to follow the reference and see what the tool can actually establish.</p></div></div></div>
    <noscript><p class="noscript-note">This explainer needs JavaScript to switch fixtures. The example above contains a dangling reference: <code>#/$defs/Location</code> has no target. Read the <a href="/work/schema-sentinel/">full case study</a> for the outcomes and CLI source.</p></noscript>
    <div class="lab-scope"><span class="mono">KNOW THE BOUNDARY</span><p>This is a browser-local teaching model of <strong>three curated fixtures</strong>, not the SchemaSentinel CLI or a general schema validator. “No findings” applies only to this example. The full project checks three documented rules and explicit unsupported-feature boundaries.</p></div>
    <div class="lab-lessons"><div><span class="mono">01 / LOOK FOR THE CAUSE</span><h3>Present isn’t usable.</h3><p>A tool can appear in a list while its input contract points to something that no longer exists.</p></div><div><span class="mono">02 / KEEP THE STATES DISTINCT</span><h3>Unknown isn’t clean.</h3><p>An external reference crosses this check’s boundary. That deserves a different outcome from a pass.</p></div><div><span class="mono">03 / MAKE THE NEXT STEP CLEAR</span><h3>A finding needs a fix.</h3><p>Show where the contract broke and what would restore it. An error code is only the beginning.</p></div></div>
  </section>
</Base>
<script>
  import '../../scripts/lab';
</script>
